            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Before the new one: say the Latin for to read through, then the Latin for to unroll."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 1,
          "type": "input",
          "title": "You'll want to know: capsa, capsae",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "capsa, capsae — \"a book box\"."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Puer librōs in capsā portat. The boy carries his books in a book box."
            },
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "Feminine, first declension, from capiō — to hold. English case, a box, comes from it through French."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 2,
          "type": "etymology",
          "title": "The word, taken apart",
          "segments": [
            {
              "kind": "speech",
              "text": "capsa is built on capiō \"to take, hold\" (Lewis & Short; EtymDB). English case \"box\" comes from it through French, and capsule from its diminutive."
            }
          ]
        },
        {
          "index": 3,
          "type": "notice",
          "title": "What you've built",
